<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2009">2009</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as AQAP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">Al-Qaeda</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> (AQAP) is a jihadist group formed in January 2009 by the merger of al-Qaeda's Saudi and Yemeni branches, based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. It plotted attacks on Western aircraft and in 2011–2012 and 2015–2016 held towns in southern Yemen, including the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a>. It has been a main target of American drone strikes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-qaeda-in-the-arabian-peninsula.jpg" alt="Al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islamic_State_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qaeda">Al-Qaeda</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, or AQAP, is a Salafi-jihadist militant organisation that seeks to overthrow the Yemeni and Saudi governments and to establish an Islamic emirate across the Arabian Peninsula. In the 2000s, al-Qaeda's Saudi branch carried out several mass-casualty attacks on civilians in Saudi Arabia. After that bombing campaign failed and its cells were dismantled, several operatives fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. AQAP was formed in January 2009 by a merger of Al-Qaeda in Yemen and Al-Qaeda in Saudi Arabia, and at the time it had no more than a few hundred members.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its first notable attacks were suicide bombings in March 2009 that targeted South Korean nationals. On 25 December 2009 Umar Farouk Abdulmutallab tried to blow up Northwest Airlines Flight 253 as it approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a>, and AQAP claimed responsibility three days later. In October 2010 two printer cartridge bombs sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> on cargo planes were found at East Midlands Airport and in Dubai, and AQAP claimed the plot on 5 November 2010.</p><p>During the Yemeni Revolution of 2011, AQAP created a local subsidiary, Ansar Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a>, which seized towns in Abyan and Shabwah in the south and declared an Islamic Emirate of Waqar. A government offensive after Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi took power in February 2012 drove it out of most of this territory. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-civil-war-2014-present">Yemeni civil war</a> began in 2014, AQAP reached its territorial peak in 2015, taking much of southern Hadhramaut, including its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a>, and fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> in Al-Bayda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Taiz</a>.</p><p>Its strength later waned through internal struggles and operations by several parties in the civil war. Attacks abroad claimed by or linked to the group included the Charlie Hebdo shooting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 7 January 2015, which AQAP claimed on 14 January, and the shooting at Naval Air Station Pensacola on 6 December 2019.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The group has an emir, who consults a Shura Council, and a general command of the emir and six to eight other leaders. A UN Security Council report of 2018 described doctrinal, propaganda, military, security and financial subdivisions. In July 2010 its Al-Malahem Media launched Inspire, an English-language magazine aimed at a Western audience. Its income came mainly from robberies, kidnappings and donations.</p><p>Since 2009 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> has led a drone campaign against it. A strike on 30 September 2011 killed the preacher Anwar Al-Awlaki and the Inspire editor Samir Khan, and further strikes killed leaders including Nasser Al-Wuhayshi in June 2015 and Qasim Al-Raymi in January 2020. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, the United States, Saudi Arabia and the UAE, among others, have designated it a terrorist organisation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Militant jihadism opened a further front: al-Qaeda bombed the USS Cole in Aden harbour in October 2000, killing seventeen American sailors, and <b>Al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula</b>, formed in 2009, became a target of American drone strikes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Military</a></li></ul></section>
